    "_meanings": [
        ".²1 An oblation, a gift or offering (usually religious); {#nIvArabaliM#}\n {#vilokayataH#} <ls>Ś. 4. 21</ls>; <ls>U. 1. 50</ls>.\n",
        ".²2 The offering of a portion of the daily meal of rice, grain, ghee <ab>&c.</ab>\n to all creatures, (also called {#BUtayajYa#}), one of the five daily Yajñas\n to be performed by a householder; see <ls>Ms. 3. 67</ls>, <ls n=\"Ms. 3.\">91</ls>; it is usually performed\n by throwing up into the air, near the house-door, portions of the daily\n meal before partaking of it; {#yAsAM baliH sapadi madgfhadehalInAM haMsESca#}\n {#sArasagaRESca viluptapUrvaH#} <ls>Mk. 1. 9</ls>.\n",
        ".²3 Worship, adoration; <ls>Rām. 2. 3. 8</ls>; {#avacitabalipuzpA vedisaMmArgadakzA#}\n <ls>Ku. 1. 60</ls>; <ls>Me. 57</ls>; {#avacitAni balikarmaparyAptAni puzpARi#} <ls>Ś. 4</ls>.\n",
        ".²4 Fragments of food left at a meal.\n",
        ".²5 A victim offered to a deity.\n",
        ".²6 A tax, tribute, impost; also ‘religious tax’; (<ab>cf.</ab> {#sItA, BAgo, baliH#},\n {#karo … … ca rAzwram#}); <ls>Kau. A. 2. 6. 24</ls>; {#prajAnAmeva BUtyarTaM#}\n {#sa tAByo balimagrahIt#} <ls>R. 1. 18</ls>; <ls>Ms. 7. 80</ls>, <ls n=\"Ms.\">8. 307</ls>;\n\n {#prajiGAya baliM taTA#} <ls>Śiva B. 29. 42</ls>; {#na cAjihIrzIMd balimapravfttam#} <ls>Bu. Ch. 2. 44</ls>.\n",
        ".²7 The handle of a {%chowrie%}.\n",
        ".²8 <ab>N.</ab> of a celebrated demon; {#yena badDo balI rAjA dAnavendro mahAbalaH#}\n <ls>Rakṣābandhanamantra.</ls> [He was a son of Virochana, the son of Prahlāda.\n He was a very powerful demon and oppressed the gods very much. They, therefore,\n prayed to Viṣṇu for succour, who descended on earth as a son of Kaśyapa\n and Aditi in the form of a dwarf. He assumed the dress of a mendicant,\n and having gone to Bali prayed him to give him as much earth as he could\n cover in three steps. Bali, who was noted for his liberality, unhesitatingly\n acceded to this apparently simple request. But the dwarf soon assumed\n a mighty form, and began to measure the three steps. The first step covered\n the earth, the second the heavens; and not knowing where to place the\n third, he planted it on the head of Bali and sent him and all his legions\n to the Pātāla and allowed him to be its ruler. Thus the universe was\n once more restored to the rule of Indra; <ab>cf.</ab> {#Calayasi vikamaRe balimadButavAmana#}\n <ls>Gīt. 1</ls>; <ls>R. 7. 35</ls>; <ls>Me. 59</ls>. Viṣṇu is said to still guard his door in\n Pātāla. He is one of the seven {%Chirajivins%}; <ab>cf.</ab> {#cirajIvin#}].\n"
    ],
